    <string name="tomato_heat_alert">Heat stress possible. Ensure adequate watering and consider providing afternoon shade.</string>
    <string name="tomato_cold_alert">Risk of chilling injury. Protect young plants if temperatures drop further.</string>
    <string name="tomato_humidity_alert">High humidity and rain increase the risk of fungal diseases like Late Blight. Ensure good air circulation.</string>

    <string name="rice_heat_alert">High temperatures can affect pollen viability. Ensure paddy is adequately flooded.</string>
    <string name="rice_cold_alert">Cold temperatures can slow growth. Maintain optimal water level to buffer temperature.</string>
    <string name="rice_transplanting_advice">Rainy conditions are ideal for transplanting rice seedlings.</string>
    <string name="rice_water_advice">Maintain consistent water levels in the paddy. Lack of rain may require increased irrigation.</string>

    <string name="wheat_heat_alert">Heat stress during grain filling can reduce yield. Ensure soil moisture is adequate.</string>
    <string name="wheat_frost_alert">Frost can damage flowering and grain development. Light irrigation may help if frost is predicted.</string>
    <string name="wheat_humidity_alert">High humidity increases the risk of rust diseases. Scout fields regularly.</string>

    <string name="maize_heat_alert">Extreme heat can interfere with pollination. Ensure good soil moisture to manage stress.</string>
    <string name="maize_cold_alert">Cold temperatures can stunt growth, especially in early stages.</string>
    <string name="maize_drainage_advice">Heavy rain can lead to waterlogging. Ensure good field drainage.</string>

    <string name="cotton_heat_alert">High temperatures can cause square and boll drop. Maintain consistent irrigation.</string>
    <string name="cotton_cold_alert">Cool temperatures can slow vegetative growth.</string>
    <string name="cotton_humidity_alert">Damp conditions increase the risk of boll rot. Monitor plants closely.</string>

    <string name="remedy_healthy_plant_category">Healthy Plant</string>
    <string name="remedy_healthy_plant_cultural">Continue standard care routines: regular deep watering, balanced N-P-K fertilization according to crop stage, and routine scouting for early signs of pests.</string>
    <string name="remedy_healthy_plant_chemical">None required.</string>

    <string name="remedy_viral_category">Viral Infection</string>
    <string name="remedy_viral_cultural">Immediate removal: Uproot and burn or bag the infected plants (do not compost them). Disinfect all tools and wash hands thoroughly after handling to prevent spread.</string>
    <string name="remedy_viral_chemical">No cure for the virus itself. Treat the insect vectors (like whiteflies) using Imidacloprid or Dinotefuran to prevent them from spreading the disease to healthy plants.</string>

    <string name="remedy_greening_category">Systemic Bacterial Disease</string>
    <string name="remedy_greening_cultural">There is no cure for Citrus Greening. Infected trees will rapidly decline and must be entirely removed and destroyed to protect neighboring citrus groves.</string>
    <string name="remedy_greening_chemical">Focus strictly on vector control. Use systemic insecticides like Imidacloprid applied to the soil to control the Asian citrus psyllid population.</string>

    <string name="remedy_bacterial_spot_category">Bacterial Infection</string>
    <string name="remedy_bacterial_spot_cultural">Avoid working in the garden when plants are wet to prevent spreading the bacteria. Rotate crops annually. Sanitize pruning tools with a 10% bleach solution between cuts.</string>
    <string name="remedy_bacterial_spot_chemical">Apply Copper-based bactericides (e.g., Copper octanoate, Copper sulfate). Tank-mixing copper with Mancozeb can increase efficacy against tough bacterial spots.</string>

    <string name="remedy_mites_category">Pest Infestation</string>
    <string name="remedy_mites_cultural">Regularly spray the undersides of leaves with a strong blast of water to physically dislodge the mites and destroy their webs. Introduce natural predators like ladybugs.</string>
    <string name="remedy_mites_chemical">Use specific miticides/acaricides containing Bifenazate, Abamectin, or Spiromesifen. Avoid broad-spectrum insecticides (like Sevin), as they kill beneficial predators.</string>

    <string name="remedy_fungal_category">Fungal Infection</string>
    <string name="remedy_fungal_cultural">Prune infected leaves and stems immediately. Ensure adequate spacing between plants to improve air circulation. Water at the base of the plant using drip irrigation rather than overhead sprinklers to keep foliage dry. Clean up fallen debris.</string>
    <string name="remedy_fungal_chemical">Use Chlorothalonil (highly effective broad-spectrum for blights and spots), Captan (for scab), Mancozeb (for black rots/rusts), or Myclobutanil (for powdery mildew) depending on availability.</string>
